  "signature": "(-,+,+,+)",
  "convention": "The coordinate $r$ is the areal radius. The parameter $r_s = 2GM/c^2$ is the Schwarzschild radius and $r_q^2 = Q^2 G / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0 c^4)$ is the charge radius squared, where $Q$ is the total electric charge. The metric function is $\\Delta = r^2 - r_s r + r_q^2$. Horizons occur at $r_\\pm = \\dfrac{r_s}{2} \\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{r_s^2}{4} - r_q^2}$ when $r_s \\geq 2r_q$. When $r_s > 2r_q$ the coordinates cover three regions of the maximally extended spacetime, one of each kind: an exterior $r > r_+$, a region $r_- < r < r_+$ where $r$ is the time, and an inner region $0 < r < r_-$ where $t$ is a time again and the singularity at $r = 0$ is timelike. The metric is unchanged under $t \\to -t$, so between the horizons the coordinates cannot say whether $r$ decreases toward the future, the black hole, or increases, the white hole. Here the region between the horizons is the black hole, the one an observer falling in from the exterior enters, and the inner region lies to its future. The Ricci tensor is the standard contraction $R_{\\mu\\nu} = R^\\alpha{}_{\\mu\\alpha\\nu}$, on which the Einstein tensor is that of the radial electric field: $G^t{}_t = G^r{}_r = -\\dfrac{r_q^2}{r^4}$ carries a positive energy density and the two angular components carry the opposite sign. Units with $c = 1$ are used throughout, and components are taken in the chart $x^0 = ct$, which those units make $t$ itself, so $t$ is a length beside $r$, $r_s$ and $r_q$ and no component carries a factor of $c$. The geodesic equations are $\\ddot{x}^\\mu + \\Gamma^\\mu{}_{\\nu\\rho}\\dot{x}^\\nu\\dot{x}^\\rho = 0$ with $\\Gamma$ the Christoffel symbols of that same chart, and the dots are derivatives of that chart with respect to an affine parameter $\\lambda$, so $\\dot{t}$ means $d(ct)/d\\lambda$ and every term of every equation carries the dimensions of its left hand side.",
  "history": "The charged generalization of the Schwarzschild metric arrived in pieces, and the story parallels the uncharged case almost beat for beat. In May 1916, Hans Reissner gave the gravitational field of a point charge, solving the Einstein equations in the presence of a Coulombic electric field [reissner1916]. His derivation used Schwarzschild's auxiliary radius $R$, so the solution was not yet in its modern form, and the structure of its singularities was interpreted through the same lens that had obscured Schwarzschild's. Nordström was himself no stranger to the problem of gravity: his scalar theory of gravitation had been a serious contender before Einstein's field equations settled the matter in 1915.¶He turned to the victorious theory anyway, and in 1918 did for Reissner's metric what Droste had done for Schwarzschild's: imposing isotropy directly on the auxiliary radius, he extended the areal coordinate to $r = 0$, giving the solution its modern form [nordstrom1918]. Hermann Weyl had arrived at the same metric independently in 1917, as one member of a broad class of static fields in which the gravitational and electrostatic potentials depend on one another [weyl1917, bicak2000]. George Barker Jeffery derived it again in 1921, solving the field equations directly for the field of an electron, and learned of Nordström's work only after his own paper was written [jeffery1921].¶The metric is characterized by two length scales: the Schwarzschild radius $r_s = 2GM/c^2$ and the charge radius, defined by $$r_q^2 = \\frac{Q^2 G}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 c^4},$$ where $M$ is the mass and $Q$ the electric charge of the source. The metric admits up to two horizons, located at $$r_\\pm = \\frac{r_s}{2} \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{r_s^2}{4} - r_q^2}.$$ These coalesce when $r_s = 2r_q$ in the extremal case, and become imaginary when the ratio of charge to mass exceeds $\\sqrt{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 G}$. Nordström noted that the ratios of charge to mass of the proton and electron far exceed this threshold, and used this to argue that the regime beyond extremality represents elementary charged particles, elegantly banishing the horizons by phenomenological fiat. It wouldn't be until decades later that the case beyond extremality was understood as a naked singularity, a violation of cosmic censorship [misner1973].¶John Graves and Dieter Brill gave the analytic extension of the charged metric in 1960 and found it unlike Kruskal's: the throat of the charged wormhole contracts to a least radius and expands again after a finite proper time, pulsating, cushioned by the Maxwell pressure of the electric field through it, and no uncharged test particle can reach the singularity at $r = 0$ [graves1960]. Brandon Carter completed the extension in 1966 for the extremal case, where the charge equals the mass [carter1966]. Sudhansu Datta Majumdar generalized Weyl's work in 1947 to static fields in which the metric and the electrostatic potential are tied by a single relation, which reduces the field equations to Laplace's equation [majumdar1947], and James Hartle and Stephen Hawking showed in 1972 that such solutions extend analytically to extreme charged black holes held in equilibrium by the balance of gravity and electricity [hartle1972]. Werner Israel proved in 1968 that among static electrovacuum fields that are asymptotically flat, with closed, simply connected equipotential surfaces, the only ones with a regular event horizon are the Reissner-Nordström solutions whose mass is at least as large as their charge in natural units [israel1968].¶The Ricci scalar vanishes identically for this metric, yet the Ricci tensor is nonzero: the spacetime is sourced entirely by the energy and momentum of the electromagnetic field. The Reissner-Nordström metric is the unique static, spherically symmetric solution to the Einstein-Maxwell equations, and Einstein and Rosen later applied the same trick of extending the domain to it as they had to the Schwarzschild metric, interpreting the two sheets of the geometry as positive and negative particles. Vincent Moncrief showed in 1974 that the coupled gravitational and electromagnetic perturbations of even parity outside a Reissner-Nordström black hole have no unstable normal modes [moncrief1974].¶Michael Simpson and Roger Penrose found in 1973, from computer calculations of test electromagnetic fields, that instabilities arise at the inner horizon though not at the event horizon, and inferred that in the full theory the inner horizon becomes a curvature singularity once perturbations without symmetry are present [simpson1973]. Eric Poisson and Werner Israel made the mechanism explicit in 1989 and 1990: the slowly decaying radiative tail of a collapse is blueshifted without limit at the inner horizon and inflates the internal mass parameter of the hole without bound, while the mass seen from outside stays finite, which they called mass inflation [poisson1989, poisson1990]. Mihalis Dafermos proved in 2005 that their picture is right for a spherical charged black hole coupled to a scalar field: the spacetime extends continuously across a boundary on which the Hawking mass blows up, so it cannot be extended as a differentiable metric, and in Christodoulou's formulation by continuous extension the strong cosmic censorship conjecture fails for that system [dafermos2005].",
